  Spec ref:   putDispensedMedications_v0.2_20251224.xlsx (Synapxe), Request tab
  Generated:  2026-05-26

  SCENARIO
  =================
  Continuation of the TAN AH BENG cardiology scenario.

  Timeline:
    2026-05-12  Initial prescription written by Dr Ruth Kam (covered by the
                accompanying NEHR_putOrderedMedications_Sample_Cardiology.xml).
    2026-05-12  Initial 30-day dispense at Heart Clinic dispensary — same
                day as the consultation. (NOT shown in this file; this file
                covers the refill 30 days later.)
    2026-06-11  THIS FILE — Patient returns to Heart Clinic dispensary
                without seeing the doctor, picks up the 30-day refill of all
                three medications. dispenseType code = REF (Refill).
                The dispense references the ORIGINAL orderID from 2026-05-12.

  Why this scenario matters:
    Refill-without-visit is the case where Dispense count exceeds Order count
    over a period. The original order spans (typically) 90 days with multiple
    refills authorised; each refill collection generates a new Dispense
    message but no new Order message. NEHR uses orderID to link refills
    back to the original authorisation.

  KEY DIFFERENCES vs putOrderedMedications
  =================
  1. Wrapping grouping is <medicationDispense> not <medicationOrder>.
  2. dispenseDateTime instead of orderDateTime (the actual handover moment).
  3. dispenseID, dispensedBy, dispensingInstitution, dispensingLocation.
  4. dispenseType code distinguishes Initial (INI) vs Refill (REF).
  5. medicationItemDispensedDate per-item.
  6. References the originating order via orderID at the medicationDispense level
     (orderedBy carried through too — the prescriber is still recorded).
  7. The medicationItem block structure is identical to putOrderedMedications —
     same SDD coded triple, same SMST coded triples, same dosage/duration.

  ANNOTATION FORMAT
  =================
  Same as putOrderedMedications sample:
    [M | O | C]   Significance: Mandatory / Optional / Conditional Mandatory
    card N        Cardinality
    Data type / length
  Plus inline value justification per element.

  CODESET / SDD NOTES
  =================
  SDD ConceptIds are the SAME as the originating order — once a drug has been
  prescribed with a specific SDD code, all subsequent dispenses against that
  order should carry the identical ConceptId. This is what allows NEHR's
  reconciliation engine to match dispenses to orders.

  SMST OPT-OUT
  =================
  Same as putOrderedMedications — SMST coded triples are shown populated with
  [SMST] markers; if client opts out, omit the coded groupings and retain
  textDescription only.
================================================================================
-->

